# Home on the Range, strum and sing (repertoire plan: home-on-the-range). A new arrangement of
# the 1873 song: the first verse, with the tune as it is commonly sung today (older printings
# vary), exactly as independent recollections agreed on it note for note. The chorus is left
# out: its tune was not agreed, so it is not guessed.
# Your part: a waltz strum, the chord's root alone on beat 1, then down-up on beats 2 and 3.
# Roots: G on string 6 fret 3, C on string 5 fret 3, A7 on the open string 5, D7 on the open
# string 4. Shapes: G (320003), C (x32010), A7 (x02020), D7 (xx0212).
# The four lines of the verse take four bars each: G G C C | G G A7 D7 | G G C C | G D7 G G.
# A7 under "play" is the V of V: the dominant of D, so it pulls to D7 in the next bar, and D7,
# the dominant of G, pulls home to G. The words are written above the bar where each line
# starts, as on a chord sheet. The tune starts on beat 3 with "Oh", so you wait one beat.
# The tune (second track) is played an octave below the sung pitch, in first position on strings
# 4, 3 and 2.
title: Home on the Range
id: home-on-the-range
composer: Daniel E. Kelley (music); words by Brewster Higley
source: Song of 1873 (as My Western Home); melody as commonly sung today; verse 1; arranged for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain (Kelley died in 1905, Higley in 1911); this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 4
tags: strumming, waltz, 3/4, pickup, secondary dominant, lyrics
tempo: 96
time: 3/4
key: G
pickup: q

track: Your strum
role: rhythm
